# Opening a RunOS session

Do these three calls before any other RunOS tool. They are cheap. Skipping them
costs a refused call and a wasted turn.

1. `mcp_bootstrap`. Call it once per RunOS server per session. It returns the
   instructions and the index of documentation topic keys.
2. One `mcp_topics_show`, with an exact key from that index. Pick the topic that
   matches the task.
3. `cli_version-check`. Tell the user if their CLI is behind.

## Why step 2 is not optional

The RunOS read server refuses its tools until two documents have been read.
`mcp_bootstrap` counts as one. Each `mcp_topics_show` counts as one.
`mcp_topics_search` finds keys but **does not count**, so a search alone leaves you
still gated.

Search when you do not know the key. Then show the topic. The show is the one that
satisfies the gate.

## Never state a RunOS fact from memory

Every fact about RunOS comes from a topic or from a tool result. That includes
service types, size and class names, field names, file names, environment variable
names, command names and URLs.

If you cannot fetch the topic, say so and stop. Do not fill the gap from a similar
platform and do not reason from a config file you found in the repository. A
plausible wrong value is worse than an admitted gap, because the user cannot see it
is wrong.

## When a tool refuses

Read the `code` field of the error, never the prose.

If the refusal says `not authenticated`, tell the user to run `runos login` in their
terminal and wait. In a headless or background agent there is no browser, so the
route is the `RUNOS_API_KEY` environment variable instead. Do not retry.

If a tool you expect is missing, or refuses an argument the topic says it takes,
call `manifest_update` and try once more.

For the full call contract, read the `cli-mcp-contract` topic.
